==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la}} {{term|refractarius|refractārius|obstinate}}, from {{term|refractus}}, past participle of {{term|refringere||to break up}}. Originally {{term|refractary}} reanalysed after other adjectives in {{term|-ory}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/ɹɪˈfɹæk.təɹ.i/}}
* {{audio|en-us-refractory.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[obstinate|Obstinate]] and [[unruly]]; strongly [[opposed]] to something.
#*'''1787''', [[w:Alexander Hamilton|Alexander Hamilton]], "[[s:Federalist No. 16|Federalist No. 16]]", December 4,
#*:{{...}} in most instances attempts to coerce the '''refractory''' and disobedient have been the signals of bloody wars, {{...}}
#*'''1836''', [[w:Charles Dickens|Charles Dickens]], ''[[w:The Pickwick Papers|The Pickwick Papers]]'', [[s:The_Pickwick_Papers/Chapter_26|Chapter 26]],
#*:Mr. Weller knocked at the door, and after a pretty long interval—occupied by the party without, in whistling a tune, and by the party within, in persuading a '''refractory''' flat candle to allow itself to be lighted {{...}}
# Not affected by great [[heat]].
# {{medicine}} [[difficult|Difficult]] to [[treat]].
# {{biology}} [[incapable|Incapable]] of registering a [[reaction]] or [[stimulus]].

